%I A400861 #10 Oct 13 2026 16:54:49
%S A400861 0,1,3,6,8,11,16,21,26,32,37,44,52,60
%N A400861 a(n) is the largest N such that the integers 1..n can be arranged in a row so that the sums of blocks of adjacent terms (including single terms) cover every integer from 1 to N.
%C A400861 a(n) <= n*(n+1)/2, with equality only for n = 0, 1, 2, 3 among the listed terms.
%C A400861 The sequence is nondecreasing: appending n+1 to an optimal arrangement of 1..n keeps all earlier block sums.
%e A400861 a(3) = 6 via the arrangement 1, 3, 2: block sums are 1, 3, 2, 4, 5, 6.
%e A400861 a(4) = 8 via 3, 1, 4, 2: block sums are 1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7, 8, 10 (9 is missing).
%o A400861 (Python)
%o A400861 from itertools import permutations
%o A400861 def a(n):
%o A400861     best = 0
%o A400861     for p in permutations(range(1, n+1)):
%o A400861         s = set()
%o A400861         for i in range(n):
%o A400861             t = 0
%o A400861             for j in range(i, n):
%o A400861                 t += p[j]; s.add(t)
%o A400861         m = 0
%o A400861         while m+1 in s: m += 1
%o A400861         best = max(best, m)
%o A400861     return best
%Y A400861 Cf. A000217.
%K A400861 nonn,more,hard
%O A400861 0,3
%A A400861 _Dimas Saputra_, Oct 07 2026
%E A400861 a(12)-a(13) from _Sean A. Irvine_, Oct 13 2026
